course: dictionary
lesson: 12198
title: Confiscation: taken for a reason
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Confiscation is a noun. It means the act of taking something away from someone because of a rule, or as a punishment. «Phones seen in class are liable to confiscation.» The school has a rule, so a phone seen in class can be taken away.
explain: The word also tells you that the taking was official. Someone in charge made the decision, and there was a reason for it. «The court ordered the confiscation of the boat used to carry the stolen goods.» A court is in charge here, and the boat was taken because of the crime.
explain: You can spot the word by its ending, -tion. Many nouns that come from verbs end this way: a word that means to take something away becomes confiscation when you talk about the act of doing it. Check the sentence: does it sound like a rule, a punishment, or the law? If so, confiscation is probably the right meaning.
fact: Confiscation is a noun. Its plural is confiscations.
fact: «Confiscation» means the act of taking something away from someone because of a rule or as a punishment.
fact: In law, confiscation means the official taking of property by the police, a court or the government.
fact: Useful phrases include «confiscation of property», «face confiscation» and «risk confiscation».
fact: Clue to look for: if the sentence mentions a rule, a court, the police or customs, the taking is probably official.
exercise: «Phones seen in class are liable to confiscation.» What does confiscation mean here? | Being mended by a repair shop | Being given a prize for good work | *Being taken away by the school because of the rule | Being lent to a friend for the day
exercise: The customs officers made the confiscation of the smuggled watches at the airport. What does this tell you? | The watches were a present for the officers | The watches were lost by accident | *The taking was official and had a reason | The watches were sold in a shop
exercise: «The king ordered the confiscation of the farm.» Find and copy the word for the act of taking something away. | farm | king | ordered | *confiscation
test: «The judge ordered the confiscation of the car used in the crime.» What does confiscation mean? | A new coat of paint for the car | *The official taking of property by a court | A car wash after a long journey | A free ride to school
test: Which word is the plural of confiscation? | confiscationes | confiscates | confiscation's | *confiscations
exam: The school sign says: «Phones seen in class face confiscation.» Why is this taking official? | Because the pupil asked for the phone to be taken | Because the phone is a gift from the teacher | Because the phone is broken and needs fixing | *Because the school has a rule and a reason
